## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  GUIBANK.INC.BL -- the low memory front door to a banked GUI.
## **
## **      GUI.SAY   GUI.YN   GUI.MENU   GUI.TEXT
## **      GUI.OPEN  GUI.CLOSE
## **      GUI.LISTBOX
## **
## **  Seven shims, and nothing else. Each one selects the code bank and calls
## **  the body, which lives at $A000 in that bank; the bodies are the same
## **  routines under a .BODY name, in GUI.INC.BL and GUI2.INC.BL unchanged
## **  but for their label lines.
## **
## **  WHY A SHIM AT ALL: a banked routine cannot select its own bank. By the
## **  time its first instruction is fetched the wrong bank is already selected
## **  and the fetch has already read rubbish. Something in LOW memory has to
## **  set the bank before the call, and that is the whole job of this file.
## **
## **  Usage -- the caller says nothing it did not say before:
## **
## **      #INCLUDE "GUIBANK.INC.BL"        ' before the region
## **      GOTO MY.LIBEND
## **      GP.BANKED GUI.CODEBANK
## **      #INCLUDE "GUI.INC.BL"
## **      #INCLUDE "GUI2.INC.BL"
## **      GP.ENDBANKED
## **      MY.LIBEND:
## **
## **  THE GOTO OVER THE REGION IS NOT OPTIONAL. GP.BANKED leaves a bridge so
## **  that falling into the region still works, and at program start it does
## **  work -- the bootstrap copies the code up and leaves that bank selected.
## **  Relying on it means every module's GOTO ... MODULE.END runs from the
## **  bank for no reason, and stops working the day anything selects another
## **  bank before the includes are reached. Jump over it instead.
## **
## **  ONE #DEFINE, USED TWICE: GP.BANKED takes a decimal constant and #DEFINE
## **  substitutes at translation time, so GP.BANKED GUI.CODEBANK and the BANK
## **  in every shim below cannot drift apart. Claim it from BANKMGR as well,
## **  or something else will hand it out:
## **
## **      BANKMGR.WANT = GUI.CODEBANK : GOSUB BANKMGR.CLAIM
## **
## **  A GUI CALL LEAVES THE CODE BANK SELECTED, and that is safe rather than
## **  merely tolerable. Nothing in BASIC can read the bank back -- PEEK and
## **  POKE select and restore around every access -- BLOAD, BSAVE and BVERIFY
## **  take the bank as an argument, and STASH puts the caller's bank back for
## **  itself. The one thing that would care is p-code in ANOTHER bank, and the
## **  compiler allows one region a program.
## **
## **  The shims do NOT restore the caller's bank, deliberately. It would need
## **  a variable to hold it and GUI.LISTBOX calls GUI.OPEN through this file,
## **  so a single variable would be overwritten by the inner call and the
## **  outer one would put back the wrong bank -- worse than not trying.
## **
## **  GUI2's calls to GUI.OPEN and GUI.CLOSE come back through here too. Both
## **  are already in the bank, so the hop is two GOSUBs it did not need; it is
## **  left that way because the alternative is editing call sites inside a
## **  module to say .BODY, and a wrong one there fails as a hang rather than
## **  as a message.
## **
## **  The whole GUI.* name space belongs to GUI.INC.BL and GUI2.INC.BL; this
## **  file owns only GUI.CODEBANK and the seven labels above.
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF GUIBANK.DEFS
#DEFINE GUIBANK.DEFS 1

## The bank the GUI runs in. Two of the 63 a 512K machine has.
#DEFINE GUI.CODEBANK 4

GOTO GUIBANK.MODULE.END

GUI.SAY:
    BANK GUI.CODEBANK
    GOSUB GUI.SAY.BODY
    RETURN

GUI.YN:
    BANK GUI.CODEBANK
    GOSUB GUI.YN.BODY
    RETURN

GUI.MENU:
    BANK GUI.CODEBANK
    GOSUB GUI.MENU.BODY
    RETURN

GUI.TEXT:
    BANK GUI.CODEBANK
    GOSUB GUI.TEXT.BODY
    RETURN

GUI.OPEN:
    BANK GUI.CODEBANK
    GOSUB GUI.OPEN.BODY
    RETURN

GUI.CLOSE:
    BANK GUI.CODEBANK
    GOSUB GUI.CLOSE.BODY
    RETURN

GUI.LISTBOX:
    BANK GUI.CODEBANK
    GOSUB GUI.LISTBOX.BODY
    RETURN

GUIBANK.MODULE.END:
#ENDIF
